n Theorem 1 are stable for sufficiently large 𝑛.\n\nTheorem 2\n\nFor any 𝜖∈(0,1/√𝑒) and 𝑛>𝑛⁡(𝜖), any intersecting family of ℳ2⁢𝑛 of size greater than (1−1/√𝑒+𝜖)⁢(2⁢𝑛−3)!! is contained in a canonically intersecting family.\n\nOur proof technique was introduced by Ellis [6] to prove the bipartite version of our main result, originally conjectured by Cameron and Ku [2]. In the sequel [5], he showed this method can also be used to show stability results for t-intersecting families of perfect matchings of 𝐾𝑛,𝑛, that is, families such that any two members share 𝑡 edges.\n\nTheorem 2 also provides an alternative proof of the characterization of the extremal families in Theorem 1 for sufficiently large 𝑛; however, one can obtain a characterization holding for all 𝑛 using polyhedral techniques [10], [14]. It was thought that these polyhedral techniques could be extended to the problem of characterizing the extremal 𝑡-intersecting families of perfect matchings of 𝐾𝑛,𝑛 [8, Theorem 27], but this approach has recently been proven incorrect [9]. This refutation has sparked renewed interest in Ellis’ method, as it currently provides the simplest proof of the following seminal result in Erdős–Ko–Rado combinatorics, that the canonically 𝑡-intersecting families of perfect matchings of 𝐾𝑛,𝑛 are the extremal 𝑡-intersecting families for sufficiently large 𝑛 [7, pg. 37].\n\nTheorem 3\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nErdős-Ko-Rado Theorems: Algebraic Approaches | PDF | Representation Theory | Mathematical Objects (https://www.scribd.com/document/423908140/Erd%C5%91s-Ko-Rado-Theorems-Algebraic-Approaches)\nciteturn2search3 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.9 years ago; Crawled: 7 months ago; maximum size are the perfect matchings that do not contain a given edge. ... An intersecting family of perfect matchings from\n(a) x ≥ 0;\n\u0017\n(b) if S = {u} for some u ∈ V (X), then e∈∂S x(e) = 1;\n\u0017\n(c) if S is an odd subset of V (X) with |S